course: dictionary
lesson: 2420
title: Cyclone: a storm with a calm eye
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cyclone is a very strong storm whose winds spin round and round a calm centre. The context tells you this: «A tropical cyclone has a calm, often clear centre called the eye, surrounded by a wall of the strongest winds.» The winds are fierce, but the middle is quiet.
explain: The same kind of storm has different names in different places. «The same kind of storm is called a hurricane in the Atlantic, a typhoon in the north-west Pacific, and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean.» So if you read about a hurricane in one place and a cyclone in another, they may be the same storm with a different name.
explain: The sentences also show how serious a cyclone is. «Big storms such as hurricanes and cyclones are forecast days ahead, so families have time to prepare.» People get warnings, so they can get ready, stay indoors and follow orders to leave if told.
fact: A cyclone is a very strong storm with winds spinning around a calm centre.
fact: «Cyclone» is the name used in the Indian Ocean and around Australia. Elsewhere, the same kind of storm is called a hurricane or a typhoon.
fact: «The eye» is the calm centre of a cyclone.
fact: In weather science, a cyclone can also mean any area of low air pressure with winds turning around it.
fact: Cyclones are forecast days ahead, so people can prepare with an emergency kit and follow evacuation orders.
exercise: «A tropical cyclone has a calm, often clear centre called the eye.» What is the calm centre of a cyclone called? | The missile | The surge | The tail | *The eye
exercise: Ben says a cyclone and a hurricane must be different storms. What does the text suggest? | *They are the same kind of storm with different names | They are completely different kinds of weather | A hurricane only happens in Japan | A cyclone is a calm, sunny day
exercise: In the sentences, where is a storm called a cyclone? | The Atlantic | *The Indian Ocean | The Sahara Desert | The north-west Pacific near Japan
test: What do the winds of a cyclone do around the centre? | *They spin around it | They stop completely | They freeze | They move in straight lines away
test: Why are cyclone warnings useful? | They make the storm weaker | They stop the rain | *They give people time to prepare and leave if told to | They make the winds quieter
exam: Read: «Before a cyclone, bring in loose garden things.» Why should you do this? | Loose things keep the storm calm | The eye of the cyclone likes garden things | *Strong winds can turn loose objects into dangerous missiles | Cyclones make garden things grow faster
